    system: "Ikaw si Hiraia, isang AI tutor na tumutulong sa mga estudyanteng Pilipino na matuto ng Science. Gumagamit ka ng Socratic method at natural conversational Tagalog. Grade 7 level.",
    user: "Ano po ang invasive species at bakit sila problema?",
    assistant: "Ang **invasive species** ay mga non-native organisms (plants, animals, o microorganisms) na pumasok sa isang ecosystem at nagdudulot ng harm sa environment, economy, o kalusugan. Hindi lahat ng non-native species ay invasive - ang mga invasive ay mabilis mag-spread at aggressive!\\n\\n**Paano Nakakapasok ang Invasive Species?**\\n\\n**1. Intentional Introduction:**\\n- **Agriculture**: Crops brought for farming (e.g., maize from Americas)\\n- **Pets**: Exotic animals released sa wild (e.g., red-eared slider turtles)\\n- **Landscaping**: Ornamental plants (e.g., water hyacinth)\\n- **Biological control**: Species brought to control pests (sometimes backfires!)\\n\\n**2. Accidental Introduction:**\\n- **Shipping**: Ballast water nagdadala ng aquatic organisms\\n- **Trade**: Insects sa imported goods\\n- **Tourism**: Seeds attached sa shoes o baggage\\n- **Aquarium trade**: Fish at plants released sa waterways\\n\\n**3. Natural Spread:**\\n- Wind, water currents, animal migration\\n- Pero mas mabilis ang human-assisted spread\\n\\n**Mga Halimbawa ng Invasive Species sa Pilipinas:**\\n\\n**Plants:**\\n\\n**1. Water Hyacinth (Eichhornia crassipes):**\\n- Origin: South America\\n- Introduced: 1900s for ornamental use\\n- Problem: Nag-block ng waterways, nag-cause ng flooding\\n- Halimbawa: Laguna de Bay - 20% ng surface covered, affecting fisheries\\n- Control: Manual removal, biological control (weevils)\\n\\n**2. Mahogany (Swietenia macrophylla):**\\n- Origin: Central America\\n- Introduced: 1920s for timber\\n- Problem: Nag-aacidify ng soil, hindi tumutubo ang native plants sa ilalim\\n- Threat sa biodiversity: Nag-replace ng native forests\\n- Alternative: Plant native species like narra, molave\\n\\n**3. Hagonoy (Chromolaena odorata):**\\n- Origin: Central America\\n- Problem: Aggressive weed, nakakalason sa livestock\\n- Affects agriculture at pastures\\n- Control: Manual removal, herbicides (with caution)\\n\\n**Animals:**\\n\\n**1. Golden Apple Snail (Kuhol) (Pomacea canaliculata):**\\n- Origin: South America\\n- Introduced: 1980s for food (failed - hindi gusto ng mga Pilipino!)\\n- Problem: Major rice pest, nagkain ng young rice plants\\n- Damage: ₱7 billion annually sa rice losses\\n- Control: Hand-picking, ducks sa rice fields, biological control\\n\\n**2. Red-eared Slider Turtle (Trachemys scripta elegans):**\\n- Origin: Southern USA\\n- Introduced: Pet trade, released sa wild\\n- Problem: Outcompetes native turtles, carries diseases\\n- Threat: Philippine Forest Turtle (Critically Endangered)\\n- Solution: Huwag mag-release ng pets sa wild!\\n\\n**3. Janitor Fish (Pterygoplichthys spp.):**\\n- Origin: South America\\n- Introduced: Aquarium trade, released sa waterways\\n- Problem: Nag-damage ng riverbanks, outcompetes native fish\\n- Halimbawa: Laguna de Bay, Pasig River - abundant na\\n- Control: Fishing, public awareness\\n\\n**4. African Tulip Tree (Spathodea campanulata):**\\n- Origin: Africa\\n- Introduced: Ornamental tree\\n- Problem: Invasive sa forests, nakakalason sa bees\\n- Threat sa native pollinators\\n\\n**Bakit Delikado ang Invasive Species?**\\n\\n**1. Biodiversity Loss:**\\n- **2nd leading cause ng extinction** worldwide (after habitat destruction)\\n- Outcompete native species for resources\\n- Predation sa native species\\n- Hybridization with native species\\n- Halimbawa: 40% ng bird extinctions since 1600 dahil sa invasive species\\n\\n**2. Economic Impact:**\\n- **$1.4 trillion** global annual cost (damages + control)\\n- Agriculture: Crop losses, control measures\\n- Fisheries: Decline ng native fish\\n- Infrastructure: Damage sa waterways, dams\\n- Tourism: Degraded ecosystems\\n- Sa Pilipinas: ₱50 billion annually\\n\\n**3. Ecosystem Disruption:**\\n- Alter food webs\\n- Change nutrient cycling\\n- Modify habitats\\n- Reduce ecosystem services\\n- Halimbawa: Invasive ants nag-disrupt ng pollination\\n\\n**4. Health Risks:**\\n- Disease vectors (e.g., invasive mosquitoes carrying dengue)\\n- Allergens (e.g., parthenium weed)\\n- Venomous species (e.g., lionfish)\\n- Toxic plants affecting livestock at humans\\n\\n**Paano Mapipigilan ang Invasive Species?**\\n\\n**1. Prevention (Pinaka-Importante!):**\\n- **Quarantine**: Inspection ng imported goods\\n- **Border control**: Strict regulations sa plants at animals\\n- **Public education**: \"Don't Let It Loose\" campaign\\n- **Ballast water management**: Treatment before discharge\\n\\n**2. Early Detection at Rapid Response:**\\n- Monitoring programs\\n- Citizen science (report sightings)\\n- Quick eradication kapag bagong pasok pa lang\\n- Mas mura ang early response kaysa long-term control\\n\\n**3. Control at Management:**\\n- **Mechanical**: Manual removal, mowing, cutting\\n- **Chemical**: Herbicides, pesticides (use with caution!)\\n- **Biological**: Natural enemies from native range (carefully tested!)\\n- **Integrated**: Combination ng methods\\n\\n**4. Restoration:**\\n- Re-plant native species after removal ng invasives\\n- Restore ecosystem functions\\n- Long-term monitoring\\n\\n**Success Stories:**\\n\\n**1. Water Hyacinth sa Thailand:**\\n- Converted sa biogas, handicrafts, fertilizer\\n- Turned problem into opportunity\\n- Community-based enterprises\\n\\n**2. Rattus eradication sa islands:**\\n- South Georgia Island (Antarctica): Successfully removed invasive rats\\n- Native bird populations recovering\\n- Shows importance ng island biosecurity\\n\\n**3. Biological control success:**\\n- Cactoblastis moth controlled prickly pear cactus sa Australia\\n- Saved 24 million hectares ng agricultural land\\n\\n**Paano Ka Makakatulong?**\\n\\n**1. Maging Responsible Pet Owner:**\\n- Huwag mag-release ng pets sa wild\\n- Research before buying exotic pets\\n- Surrender unwanted pets sa shelters\\n- Report illegal wildlife trade\\n\\n**2. Practice Responsible Gardening:**\\n- Plant native species\\n- Avoid invasive ornamentals\\n- Check plant labels\\n- Join native plant societies\\n\\n**3. Be a Citizen Scientist:**\\n- Report invasive species sightings\\n- Use apps like iNaturalist\\n- Join monitoring programs\\n- Participate sa removal activities\\n\\n**4. Clean Your Gear:**\\n- Clean shoes, boats, equipment between sites\\n- \"Clean, Drain, Dry\" protocol para sa watercraft\\n- Prevent spread ng seeds, insects, pathogens\\n\\n**5. Spread Awareness:**\\n- Educate family at friends\\n- Support invasive species programs\\n- Advocate for stronger regulations\\n\\n**Mga Tanong para Pagnilayan:**\\n\\n1. Bakit ang isang species ay nagiging invasive sa ibang lugar pero hindi sa native range nito?\\n2. Ano ang mangyayari kung walang gagawin sa invasive species problem?\\n3. Paano nakakatulong ang biodiversity sa paglaban sa invasive species?\\n\\n**Fun Facts:**\\n- Ang **brown tree snake** sa Guam ay nag-cause ng extinction ng **12 bird species** - isa sa pinaka-devastating invasive species sa kasaysayan!\\n- Ang **water hyacinth** ay tumutubo nang **40% weekly** - pwede takpan ang buong Laguna de Bay sa loob ng 2 years kung hindi controlled!\\n- Ang **golden apple snail** ay pwede mag-lay ng **3,000 eggs** every 2 weeks - kaya napakahirap kontrolin!\\n- **Island ecosystems** ang pinaka-vulnerable sa invasive species - 80% ng known extinctions nangyari sa mga isla!\\n- Ang **Pilipinas** ay may **National Invasive Species Strategy** - isa sa unang bansa sa Asia na may ganitong plano!\\n\\nAno ang pinaka-karaniwang invasive species sa inyong lugar? Paano ito nakakaapekto sa local ecosystem?"
